Yes, here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the last space before the noun (i.e., the end of the adjectives)
  let nounIndex = words.lastIndexOf(" ");

  // Swap the positions of the first and second-to-last adjective indices
  const firstAdjIndex = words.indexOf("beautiful");
  const secondLastAdjIndex = nounIndex - words.slice(nounIndex).findIndex(word => word !== "trees") - 1;
  [words[firstAdjIndex], words[secondLastAdjIndex]] = [words[secondLastAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex]];

  // Join the modified array of words back into a string
  return words.join(" ");
}

// Example usage
console.log(switchAdjectives("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

The `switchAdjectives()` function takes a sentence as input, splits it into an array of words using the `split()` method, finds the index of the last space before the noun using `lastIndexOf()`, then swaps the positions of the first and second-to-last adjective indices using destructuring assignment. Finally, the function joins the modified array of words back into a string using the `join()` method.

Note that this implementation assumes that there is exactly one noun in the sentence, which may not always be the case. In practice, you might want to add more robustness to handle edge cases like multiple nouns or ambiguous adjectives.

