To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you need to first define the size and standard deviation of the Gaussian kernel, then convolve it with the grayscale image before applying the Sobel operators. Here's how you can modify your existing code:

```matlab
% Load input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Define Gaussian filter parameters
sigma = 3;   % Standard deviation of Gaussian kernel
kernel_size = [0; 0; 0; sigma*2+1; 0; 0; 0]; % Size of Gaussian kernel
% Compute Gaussian kernel
[gaussian_kernel, gaussian_center] = fspecial('gaussian', kernel_size, sigma);
% Convolve Gaussian filter with grayscale image
filtered_image = filter2(double(gaussian_kernel), gray_image, 'replicate');
% Define Sobel operators
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operators to filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first define the size and standard deviation of the Gaussian kernel using `kernel_size` and `sigma`, respectively. We then compute the Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial` function and convolve it with the grayscale image using the `filter2` function. After applying the Sobel operators to the filtered image, we combine the resulting edges from both directions as before.

