response:
To further improve efficiency for handling very large texts, you can consider using a disk-based storage for the text files instead of loading them entirely into memory at once. Here's how you can modify the previous implementation to use disk-based storage:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWordsDisk(fileName1, fileName2)
    text1 = textscan('%c', fopen(fileName1), 'Delimiter', '%u32'); %read text1 line by line
    text2 = textscan('%c', fopen(fileName2), 'Delimiter', '%u32'); %read text2 line by line

    %Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1{1}));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2{1}));

    %Find unique words using setdiff
    uniqueWords = setdiff(unique(words1), unique(words2));

    %Close the file pointers
    fclose(fopen(fileName1));
    fclose(fopen(fileName2));
end
```

In this modified function, `textscan` is used to read the text files line by line instead of loading them into memory all at once. The `Delimiter` option is set to `'%u32'` to specify that each character is encoded as a UTF-32 integer (4 bytes per character). This allows us to read Unicode characters without any issues. Once we have read both text files into cell arrays `text1` and `text2`, we proceed with converting the text to lowercase and splitting it into words, just like before. Finally, we use `setdiff` to find the unique words that only appear in `text1`. Note that since we're reading the text files line by line, we need to explicitly close the file pointers after we're done with them to free up resources. I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions or concerns.

