Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
To implement the bubble sort algorithm with minimal memory usage and error prevention, we'll use a simple iterative approach. Here's the code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <vector>

const int MAX_SIZE = 100;

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    bool swapped = true;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size() - 1 && swapped; ++i) {
        swapped = false;
        for (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string jsonString = R"({
      "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
    })";

    nlohmann::json obj;
    try {
        obj = nlohmann::json::parse(jsonString);
        std::vector<int> arr = obj["array"];
        bubbleSort(arr);
        std::cout << "Sorted Array: ";
        for (auto& x : arr) {
            std::cout << x << ' ';
        }
    } catch (std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

