```csharp
struct ItemPrice {
    public double Price;
    public string Name;
}

double CalculateTotalCost(ItemPrice[] prices) {
    double carPrice = prices[0].Price;
    double clothesAndBookPrice = prices[1].Price + prices[2].Price;

    // Check if car price needs to be adjusted
    if (carPrice < 2 * clothesAndBookPrice) {
        carPrice = 2 * clothesAndBookPrice;
    }

    double totalCost = carPrice + clothesAndBookPrice;
    return totalCost;
}

// Example usage
ItemPrice[] itemPrices = { new ItemPrice() { Price = 3000, Name = "Car" }, new ItemPrice() { Price = 100, Name = "Clothes" }, new ItemPrice() { Price = 20, Name = "Book" } };
double totalCost = CalculateTotalCost(itemPrices);
Console.WriteLine($"Total cost: ${totalCost:F2}");
```

The `CalculateTotalCost` function takes an array of `ItemPrice` structs as input, where each struct contains the price and name of an item. Inside the function, we first extract the prices of the car, clothes, and book from the input array. We then check if the car price needs to be adjusted to meet the given constraint. If so, we set the car price to twice the combined price of clothes and book. Finally, we calculate the total cost by adding the adjusted car price and the combined price of clothes and book.

Note that we use format strings with `:F2` to format the output to display two decimal places. You can modify this format string according to your requirements.

