Here's a possible implementation of such a function:

```javascript
function findOptionsByKey(obj, key) {
  let result = [];
  const splitKey = key.split('.');
  obj.forEach(({ [splitKey[0]]: val1, [splitKey[1]]: val2 }, index) => {
    if (val1 === undefined && val2 === undefined) return; // skip if both values are missing
    const isMatching = val1 !== undefined ? val1 === true : val2 === true;
    if (isMatching) {
      const options = { label: `${val1 || val2}`, value: index };
      result.push(options);
    }
  });
  return result;
}
```

The function takes two arguments: `obj`, which is the array of objects containing the nested properties, and `key`, which is the concatenation of the keys to search for. The function returns an array of options that match the specified key.

The function uses destructuring assignment to extract the values corresponding to the first and second levels of the key. If either value is missing, the function skips this object and moves on to the next one. Otherwise, it checks whether the value is truthy (i.e., not falsy), and adds the corresponding option to the result array if it is.

Note that this implementation assumes that the key consists of exactly two levels (dot-separated). If you need to support deeper nesting, you may need to modify the function accordingly. Also note that this implementation doesn't handle edge cases like nested arrays or other complex structures very well. In such cases, you may want to consider alternative approaches, such as recursion or regular expressions.

